    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "1. Marked word order"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Spanish word order is flexible. The neutral order is subject-verb-object, but moving a word to the front or the end puts <b>focus</b> on it."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        4.4,
        6.4,
        6.2
      ],
      "head": [
        "Effect",
        "Example",
        "English"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "subject after verb (new info)",
          "Llegó Juan.",
          "Juan arrived. (news)"
        ],
        [
          "subject after verb (question)",
          "¿Qué dijo el director?",
          "What did the director say?"
        ],
        [
          "object fronted + pronoun",
          "El libro lo compré ayer.",
          "The book, I bought it yesterday."
        ],
        [
          "object fronted for contrast",
          "Eso no lo sé.",
          "That I do not know."
        ],
        [
          "focus without pronoun",
          "DINERO necesito, no consejos.",
          "Money is what I need, not advice."
        ],
        [
          "verb first (narrative)",
          "Era una noche oscura y fría.",
          "It was a dark, cold night."
        ],
        [
          "adverb fronted",
          "Ayer por la noche vimos una película.",
          "Last night we watched a film."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "dutch",
      "title": "Dutch learners",
      "text": "Dutch uses verb-second order after an adverb (<i>Gisteren zag ik hem</i>). Spanish does not invert: <i>Ayer lo vi</i>, not *Ayer vi yo lo."
    },
